        "status": "success",
        "rationale": "**Obligations.** (1) A node read builds a `Snapshot` from the contents `Domain::node_content` returned, plus those contents' `NodeContents::fingerprint`. (2) It does this without another read. (3) `Snapshot` has public `content` and `fingerprint` fields, `Snapshot::new(content, fingerprint)`, and `Clone` and `Debug` impls bounded on `T`. The `#[non_exhaustive]` attribute and the derived trait impls are structural. The `{cite}` of `requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint` is context, so its own behavior is not reassessed here.\n\n**Coverage.**\n- **Content and fingerprint pairing on reads.**\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions]` calls `read_snapshot` and asserts `.fingerprint == fp(\"before\")`. It then changes the underlying content and asserts that the recorded dependencies keep the \"before\" and \"after\" versions distinct.\n  - `#fn[direct_reads_return_complete_unicode_contents_and_snapshots]` covers an empty string and a large multi-byte Unicode string. It asserts `snapshot.content.format() == original` and `snapshot.fingerprint == fp(original)`. After the source is replaced, the snapshot still holds the original content.\n- **Fingerprint comes from the same retained contents, with no extra read.**\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot]` reads a snapshot and then changes the backing content. It asserts `snapshot.content.fingerprint() == snapshot.fingerprint`, that the content still formats as \"before\", and that the backing read log is exactly `[1]`. A re-read would break these assertions.\n  - `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk]` checks that retained contents keep their fingerprint after the source changes.\n- **Constructor and public fields.**\n  - `contracts.rs#fn[callers_can_pair_content_with_its_fingerprint]` is an external-crate test. It calls `Snapshot::new` and asserts both public fields. Using the constructor rather than a struct literal is consistent with `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n- **Use in agent paths.**\n  - `agent/tests.rs#fn[graph_tools_record_delivered_snapshots_and_prepared_pages_do_not_record_delivery]` and `#fn[agent_edge_tools_record_selections_without_validating_targets]` check the recorded node dependencies and fingerprints. Examples are `Dependency::Node(.., fingerprint.value == \"x\".repeat(18_000))`.\n- **Structure.**\n  - `public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks the exported inventory.\n  - `src/graph.rs#struct[Snapshot]` uses `#[derive(Clone, Debug)]`. Those impls have no conditional logic and no transformation of their own, so they need no separate behavioral test.\n\nI found no requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept. The tests cover the pairing of content with its fingerprint, the single read, and the constructor and fields. They have no ambiguity or marking gaps for this requirement."
      }
